  "_about": [
    "UK AND US INCOME DO NOT SHARE A CANONICAL, DELIBERATELY. The canonical list",
    "below holds dollar bands AND pound bands, and the currency symbol in each label",
    "is the signal: a US row and a UK row are not comparable on this field and must",
    "not be made to look as though they are.",
    "",
    "Converting between them was considered and rejected on 5 Oct 2026, for four",
    "reasons, any one of which would be enough:",
    "",
    "1. Market exchange rates are not purchasing power. $50,000 is about \u00a338,000 at",
    "   2016 rates, but the American pays for health insurance and often college and",
    "   the Briton does not. Same number, different life.",
    "2. There is no defensible date. GBP/USD ran 2.00 in 2007 and 1.21 in 2022, and",
    "   the TESS layer spans 2003-2024. Any single rate misstates most of the corpus.",
    "3. Inflation. A 2004 study's '$50-75K' and a 2021 study's '$50-75K' are different",
    "   real incomes; converting currency without deflating does half the job while",
    "   looking like it did all of it.",
    "4. DECISIVE: both sides are BANDS, and converted bands do not nest. \u00a310,000-",
    "   \u00a314,999 becomes roughly $13,000-$19,500, straddling two US boundaries. A",
    "   canonical category must be a union of source categories, and overlapping",
    "   bands cannot form one.",
    "",
    "What WOULD be comparable is position relative to each country's own median \u2014",
    "a top-quintile Briton and a top-quintile American are alike in a way \u00a338,000 and",
    "$50,000 never are. That belongs in a separate `income_position` field derived",
    "from national statistics per country per study year, not in this one. PMV0TG's",
    "own High/Low is already that concept, at a granularity too coarse to reuse.",
    "",
    "Within the UK the field was nearly free: two YouGov schemes sharing thirteen",
    "identical bands, differing only at the top."
  ]
}
